---
import DocPage from '../../layouts/DocPage.astro';
import Panel from '../../components/Panel.astro';
import { API_BASE, DATASET, endpoints, exampleCurl } from '../../data/api';

const ceremony = endpoints[1];
const person = endpoints[2];
---

<DocPage
  title="Getting started"
  description="Take a key, put it in the path, ask for a ceremony. The three-minute introduction to the free UrActor Academy Awards API."
  label="The basics — I"
>
  <p class="lede">
    This API serves Academy Awards data: {DATASET.ceremonies} ceremonies from {DATASET.firstYear} to {
      DATASET.lastYear
    }, every nominee and every winner. It is free, read-only, and returns plain JSON.
  </p>

  <h2 id="key">1. Take a key</h2>
  <p>
    Go to the <a href="/account">key page</a>, sign in with an email address, and a key is issued to
    you on the spot. There is no application form and nothing to approve.
  </p>

  <h2 id="path">2. Put the key in the path</h2>
  <p>
    This is the one genuinely unusual thing about this API, so it is worth saying plainly: the key
    goes in the <strong>path</strong>, not in a query string and not in a header. Every route ends
    with a <code>apikey=</code> segment.
  </p>

  <Panel label="The shape of every request" code={`${API_BASE}/{resource}/apikey=YOUR_API_KEY`} />

  <div class="aside">
    <span class="label">Because of that</span>
    <p>
      Your key will appear in server logs, browser history and referrer headers like any other part
      of a URL. Treat it as a low-value credential: it grants read-only access to public awards data
      and nothing else. If it leaks, <a href="/account">rotate it</a>.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="request">3. Ask for a ceremony</h2>
  <p>
    The most useful starting point is a single ceremony, which returns every category presented that
    year.
  </p>

  <Panel label="cURL" code={exampleCurl(ceremony)} />

  <p>
    You get back an array of categories, each holding its nominations. A nomination records who or
    what was nominated (<code>primary</code>), the context it was nominated for (<code>secondary</code
    >), and whether it won.
  </p>

  <Panel label="200 OK (truncated)" code={ceremony.exampleResponse} />

  <h2 id="search">Looking something up</h2>
  <p>
    If you already know the name you are after, go straight to it. Names are matched exactly, so
    spell them as the Academy does.
  </p>

  <Panel label="cURL" code={exampleCurl(person)} />

  <hr />

  <h2 id="next">Where to go next</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <a href="/docs/data-model">The data model</a> &mdash; what <code>primary</code> and <code
        >secondary</code
      > actually mean, and why it changes by category.
    </li>
    <li>
      <a href="/docs/authentication">Authentication</a> &mdash; keys, rotation, and what a 403 means.
    </li>
    <li><a href="/reference">The reference</a> &mdash; all six endpoints in detail.</li>
    <li><a href="/docs/limits">Limits and fair use</a> &mdash; please cache.</li>
  </ul>

  <nav class="pager" aria-label="Guides">
    <span></span>
    <a href="/docs/authentication" style="text-align:right">
      <span class="dir">Next</span>
      Authentication
    </a>
  </nav>
</DocPage>
